        <!-- Key Questions Column -->
        <div class="questions-section">
          <h2 class="section-title">Core Research Themes</h2>
          
          <div class="glass-card question-card" id="question-1">
            <div class="question-title">How do chromatin modifier complexes establish heterochromatin?</div>
            <p class="news-excerpt">We analyze how complexes like PRC2 interact with RNA molecules and specific histone modifications to silence target genomic sites.</p>
          </div>
          
          <div class="glass-card question-card alt" id="question-2">
            <div class="question-title">How do homologous chromosomes pair and interact?</div>
            <p class="news-excerpt">Using state-of-the-art cryo-ET and cryo-FIB milling, we capture the physical organization of synaptonemal complexes directly in situ.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="glass-card question-card" id="question-3">
            <div class="question-title">What regulates structural variations of gene silencing enzymes?</div>
            <p class="news-excerpt">We use mass spectrometry and biochemistry to examine how active transcription post-translational modifications inhibit and fine-tune silencing activity.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ==========================================
         RESEARCH SECTION
         ========================================== -->
    <section class="tab-section" id="research">
      <h2 class="section-title">Research Priorities</h2>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 40px; color: var(--color-text-muted); max-width: 800px;">
        Our laboratory sits at the intersection of structural biology, molecular biochemistry, and epigenetics. We develop molecular models to explain the chromatin-level regulatory pathways that govern cellular development, silencing stability, and disease states.
      </p>

      <div class="research-grid" id="research-pillars">
        <!-- Pillar 1 -->
        <div class="glass-card research-card" id="pillar-epigenetics">
          <div class="research-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-dna"></i></div>
          <h3>Epigenetics & Gene Silencing</h3>
          <p>
            We focus on Polycomb group proteins (PRC1 & PRC2) and their chromatin modifier pathways. Using single-particle cryo-EM, we visualize these massive complexes engaged with dinucleosomes to resolve their mechanism of catalysis, recruitment, and inhibition by active transcriptional modifications.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Pillar 2 -->
        <div class="glass-card research-card alt" id="pillar-tomography">
          <div class="research-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-microscope"></i></div>
          <h3>In Situ Chromosome Architecture</h3>
          <p>
            To understand how chromosomes organize in three dimensions within the nucleus, we utilize cryo-focused ion beam (cryo-FIB) milling and cryo-electron tomography (cryo-ET). This allows us to map higher-order chromatin folding and pairing interactions directly inside intact cellular environments.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Pillar 3 -->
        <div class="glass-card research-card" id="pillar-biochemistry">
          <div class="research-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-flask"></i></div>
          <h3>Biochemistry & Mass Spectrometry</h3>
          <p>
            We complement our structural studies with quantitative biochemistry, chromatin reconstitution assays, and quantitative mass spectrometry. This combined toolkit allows us to measure interaction binding affinities, kinetic behaviors, and map post-translational modifications of complex protein assemblies.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Detail Card with generated asset background -->
      <div class="glass-card" style="margin-top: 50px; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 40px; align-items: center;" id="research-methodology">
        <div>
          <h3 style="margin-bottom: 15px; font-size: 1.5rem;">Visualizing Biological Architectures</h3>
          <p style="color: var(--color-text-muted); font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            By integrating molecular resolution cryo-EM structure determination with cellular scale cryo-ET mapping, our lab bridges the gap between atomic configurations and physiological chromosome folding.
          </p>
          <ul style="list-style-type: none; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; font-size: 0.9rem;">
            <li><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-check" style="color: var(--color-primary); margin-right: 8px;"></i> Single-particle cryo-EM reconstitution of epigenetic assemblies</li>
            <li><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-check" style="color: var(--color-primary); margin-right: 8px;"></i> In situ cellular tomography with cryo-FIB grid sectioning</li>
            <li><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-check" style="color: var(--color-primary); margin-right: 8px;"></i> Reconstituted chromatin templates with designer modifications</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <img src="/assets/cryo_em_microscope.png" alt="Cryo-EM electron microscope concept graphic" style="width: 100%; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border-color); box-shadow: var(--shadow-glow-violet);">
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

